00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.600
Speaker 1: First of all, Jesus was not a white man.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.280
Speaker 1: He was not European.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.120
Speaker 1: Jesus was Middle Eastern.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:42.880
Speaker 1: The patriarchs of faith were Middle Eastern.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:53.480
Speaker 1: And by the providence of God he chose that they will be the custodians of the knowledge of God as it now became, you know, widespread to the gentiles.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:04:03.490
Speaker 1: But even going from early scripture, we see different points where Africans have always been part of the faith.

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:03.970
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:06.050
Speaker 1: I think there is Simon who is the siren.

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:08.850
Speaker 1: Siren who was part of the apostles.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:13.330
Speaker 2: There was the siren black man who carried the cross.

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:14.050
Speaker 1: The cross.

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:18.690
Speaker 1: So these are people who were part of the early church then.

00:04:19.089 --> 00:04:26.119
Speaker 1: Noteworthy is the Ethiopian union in the book of Acts, chapter 26, verse 39.

00:04:26.359 --> 00:04:28.239
Speaker 1: So he's reading the Bible by himself.

00:04:28.239 --> 00:04:34.199
Speaker 1: And it shows you the sincerity to tell you this man was top in Ethiopian in the Ethiopian order.

00:04:34.679 --> 00:04:37.039
Speaker 1: But he confronts and sees the reality.

00:04:37.039 --> 00:04:46.479
Speaker 1: I think it begs the question of how some of us are not as honest to confront the reality of the problems within our own religion or our own culture.

00:04:46.479 --> 00:04:47.159
Speaker 2: Think about it.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:05:01.520
Speaker 2: A utopian was reading Isaiah, meaning even though he had a traditional religion, he was open enough to read and question what he believed.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:08.560
Speaker 2: And I think that is one thing that many African traditional religion adherents refuse to do.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:14.160
Speaker 2: The sense of because my forefathers did this, then I must redo it.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:21.990
Speaker 2: And you said something earlier about before we started this conversation, that culture actually evolves.

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:22.470
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:37.990
Speaker 2: For example, a very good example in we mentioned earlier how that there was a culture of the Abo Bakus whereby human sacrifice was being done and few human beings were buried, sometimes buried alive with the kings.

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:39.830
Speaker 2: Now they've stopped it.

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:46.180
Speaker 2: Meaning the fact that something was done by your asses ancestors doesn't mean you continue.

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:49.500
Speaker 1: There is an objective sense of truth that can confront it.

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:54.940
Speaker 1: And it is possible for someone to maintain the good in his culture and discard of what is wrong.

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:55.420
Speaker 1: Exactly.

00:05:55.420 --> 00:06:01.020
Speaker 1: There are people who can be strong, connected to the African roots, more powerful in the knowledge of Christ.

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:01.460
Speaker 2: Yes.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:10.350
Speaker 1: And this Ethiopian eunuch I think is someone, I dare say someone that, yes, he epitomized that Christ characteristic.

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:13.110
Speaker 1: And this continues on and on where we see.

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:16.630
Speaker 1: Okay, let's look at North Africa between the first and the fourth century.

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:18.710
Speaker 1: There was nothing like slave trade.

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:22.750
Speaker 1: The dominance of the European empire had not even come at all.

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:27.150
Speaker 1: But we see strong theology and church leaders from Africa.

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:35.110
Speaker 1: People like August, St. Augustine of Hippo, Athanasius of Alexandria and Tertullian, very popular theologians.

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:42.400
Speaker 1: So, so these are some of the things I would say for you to confront the fact that Christianity is not the white man's religion.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:07:02.110
Speaker 1: By the providence of God they re emphasized it, I wouldn't say brought it, reemphasized it in Africa because history shows that people like the Ethiopian eunuch brought the gospel to Africa, but by the prominence of God they did, but the truth of the gospel that existed prior to the coming of the white.

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:09.590
Speaker 2: And think about the fact that not only did we have Christianity in Africa, Christianity was a core part of northern Africa.

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:19.350
